1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,904
This is ECE 358, video 22 b. The topic is multiple access protocols, part 2: CSMA/CD and taking turns.

2
00:00:12,354 --> 00:00:32,621
After this video, you can explain CSMA, CSMA/CD and binary exponential backoff. You can calculate backoff times and the CSMA/CD efficiency. And you can explain polling and token passing, and compare the three classes of MAC protocols.

3
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:41,668
ALOHA transmits without listening. CSMA, carrier sense multiple access, listens first. Here, A is transmitting.

4
00:00:42,118 --> 00:00:50,054
B gets a frame while A is transmitting. B senses the channel. It is busy, so B defers its transmission.

5
00:00:50,504 --> 00:00:55,112
When B senses the channel idle, it transmits its entire frame.

6
00:00:55,562 --> 00:01:13,909
CSMA/CD adds collision detection. A node also listens while it transmits. It detects a collision within a short time, and aborts. This reduces the wasted channel time. Detection is easy on a wire, but difficult on a wireless link.

7
00:01:14,359 --> 00:01:26,796
Can collisions occur with carrier sensing? Yes. This is a space-time diagram. Distance runs across, with nodes A, B, C and D on one cable. Time runs down.

8
00:01:27,246 --> 00:01:39,342
At time t zero, B senses the channel idle and starts to transmit. Its signal spreads to the left and to the right. The slope of the edges shows the propagation delay.

9
00:01:39,792 --> 00:01:49,883
At time t one, D senses the channel. B's first bits have not reached D yet. So D senses the channel idle, and D also transmits.

10
00:01:50,333 --> 00:02:03,069
Where the two signals overlap, there is a collision. It is red. Without collision detection, both nodes send their entire frames. So the transmission time of both frames is wasted.

11
00:02:03,519 --> 00:02:16,724
The propagation delay causes this. A larger distance gives a longer time in which a node cannot hear another node's frame. So distance and propagation delay set the collision probability.

12
00:02:17,174 --> 00:02:25,238
Here is the same situation with CSMA/CD. B starts at t zero, and D starts at t one.

13
00:02:25,688 --> 00:02:41,560
D detects the collision when B's first bits arrive at D. D aborts. B detects the collision when D's bits arrive at B, and B aborts. Compare the red area with the last slide. The wasted time is much shorter.

14
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:52,975
A collision at a receiver wastes frame transmissions. To reduce the wasted time, the sender must detect the collision. Then it can abort its transmission early.

15
00:02:53,425 --> 00:03:13,265
Here is the Ethernet CSMA/CD algorithm. Step 1: the NIC gets a datagram from the network layer and creates a frame. Step 2: the NIC senses the channel. If it is idle, it starts to transmit. If it is busy, it waits until the channel is idle, then transmits.

16
00:03:13,715 --> 00:03:19,774
Step 3: if the NIC sends the entire frame without a collision, it is done with this frame.

17
00:03:20,224 --> 00:03:26,944
Step 4: if the NIC detects another transmission while it sends, it aborts and sends a jam signal.

18
00:03:27,394 --> 00:03:50,093
Step 5: binary exponential backoff. After the m-th collision, the NIC chooses K at random from 0 to 2 to the power m, minus 1. It waits K times 512 bit times, and goes back to step 2. After the first collision, K is 0 or 1. After the second, K is 0 to 3.

19
00:03:50,543 --> 00:04:05,689
After the third collision, K is 0 to 7, and after the fourth, 0 to 15. So more collisions give a longer backoff interval. With more choices, two nodes are less likely to choose the same K again.

20
00:04:06,139 --> 00:04:22,331
Slide 35 shows CSMA/CD as a flowchart. An IP packet arrives from the upper layer, and the counter i starts at 0. The NIC senses the medium. If it is busy, the NIC waits and senses again.

21
00:04:22,781 --> 00:04:32,275
If the medium is not busy, the NIC transmits the frame, and it detects collisions while it transmits. With no collision, the result is success.

22
00:04:32,725 --> 00:04:41,343
With a collision, the NIC sends a jamming signal and aborts. Then it adds 1 to i, which counts the collisions of this frame.

23
00:04:41,793 --> 00:04:59,927
If i is larger than K max, the result is an error. Otherwise, the NIC chooses a random number R from 0 to 2 to the power i, minus 1. It waits R times T P, then senses the medium again. This loop is the exponential backoff.

24
00:05:00,377 --> 00:05:22,051
Slide 36 gives the parameters. The NIC senses the medium for 96 bit times. The jamming signal is 48 bits. T P is 512 bit times. One bit time is 1 over the rate: at 1 Mbps, it is 1 microsecond. The counter i stops growing at 10.

25
00:05:22,501 --> 00:05:37,328
Here are the backoff times in numbers, for a 1 Mbps link. One bit time is 1 over the rate, which is 1 microsecond. So 512 bit times is 512 microseconds.

26
00:05:37,778 --> 00:05:45,586
After the first collision, K is 0 or 1. The node waits 0 or 512 microseconds.

27
00:05:46,036 --> 00:05:57,364
After the second collision, K is 0 to 3. The longest wait is 3 times 512, which is 1536 microseconds.

28
00:05:57,814 --> 00:06:20,833
After the third collision, K is 0 to 7. The longest wait is 7 times 512, which is 3584 microseconds. The interval doubles after each collision. At the limit, i equals 10, K can be up to 1023. Then the longest wait is about point 5 2 seconds.

29
00:06:21,283 --> 00:06:33,379
Question 42 asks for the behavior of a CSMA/CD receiver, as a flowchart. Slide 37 gives it in words. Pause the video and try it.

30
00:06:33,829 --> 00:06:40,613
When a frame arrives, the receiver checks if it was received correctly. It verifies the CRC.

31
00:06:41,063 --> 00:06:53,585
If the CRC is valid, the receiver delivers the data in the frame to the protocol above, for example IP. If the CRC is invalid, it drops the frame.

32
00:06:54,035 --> 00:07:05,235
The receiver sends no ACK. The sender does not learn what the receiver did with the frame. Recovery of a lost frame is left to upper layers, such as TCP.

33
00:07:05,685 --> 00:07:26,891
Slide 38 gives the efficiency of CSMA/CD. t prop is the maximum propagation delay between two nodes in the LAN. t trans is the time to transmit a frame of the maximum size. The efficiency is 1 divided by 1 plus 5 t prop over t trans.

34
00:07:27,341 --> 00:07:45,474
The plot shows the efficiency against the ratio t prop over t trans. With example values: at a ratio of point 1, the efficiency is 1 over 1 point 5, which is point 6 7. At a ratio of 1, it is only point 1 7.

35
00:07:45,924 --> 00:08:01,796
The efficiency goes to 1 when t prop goes to 0, or when t trans goes to infinity. That is, a short cable and long frames give high efficiency. A collision is then found quickly, compared to the frame time.

36
00:08:02,246 --> 00:08:12,123
When t prop is small compared to t trans, CSMA/CD is much better than ALOHA. And it is simple, cheap and decentralized.

37
00:08:12,573 --> 00:08:28,232
Compare the classes at low load and at high load. Channel partitioning is efficient and fair at high load. But at low load, a single active node still gets only 1 over N of the bandwidth, and it must wait for its slot.

38
00:08:28,682 --> 00:08:37,066
Random access is the opposite. At low load, one node can use the full channel. At high load, collisions waste time.

39
00:08:37,516 --> 00:08:49,761
Taking turns protocols look for the best of both: no collisions at high load, and a busy node can use more of the channel. The slide adds: kind of. They have their own costs.

40
00:08:50,211 --> 00:09:00,857
The first taking turns protocol is polling. It uses a master and slaves. The master invites slave 1 to transmit. Slave 1 sends its data.

41
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:16,560
Then the master polls slave 2, and slave 2 sends its data. The master continues in turn. Polling is typically used with simple devices, such as earbuds or a wireless mouse. Bluetooth is an example.

42
00:09:17,010 --> 00:09:31,261
There are three concerns. Each poll message is overhead. A node must wait for its poll, which adds latency. And if the master fails, no node can transmit. The master is a single point of failure.

43
00:09:31,711 --> 00:09:47,775
The second taking turns protocol is token passing. A control message, the token, passes from one node to the next in sequence. Node 1 holds the token, but it has nothing to send. So it passes the token to node 2.

44
00:09:48,225 --> 00:09:57,995
Only the node that holds the token can transmit. Here, node 3 holds the token and sends its data. Then it passes the token on.

45
00:09:58,445 --> 00:10:15,597
There are three concerns: the token is overhead, nodes wait for the token, and a corrupted token stops the ring. IBM Token Ring from 1985 used this. It gave a collision-free LAN, but switched Ethernet has replaced it.

46
00:10:16,047 --> 00:10:28,463
Here is the summary of the MAC protocols. Channel partitioning divides the channel by time, frequency or code. This video covered time division and frequency division.

47
00:10:28,913 --> 00:10:51,996
Random access includes ALOHA, slotted ALOHA, simple CSMA and CSMA/CD. Carrier sensing is easy on a wire, but hard on wireless links. Ethernet used CSMA/CD. WiFi, I E E E 802 dot 11, uses CSMA/CA, collision avoidance.

48
00:10:52,446 --> 00:11:01,470
Taking turns includes polling from a central site and token passing. Examples are Bluetooth, F D D I and token ring.

49
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:09,835
Questions 39 to 41 are about CSMA and CSMA/CD. Pause the video and try it.

50
00:11:10,285 --> 00:11:34,221
Question 39. In simple CSMA, a node senses the channel before it transmits. If idle, it sends the entire frame. If busy, it defers. Question 40. Draw the flowchart from slide 35: sense, transmit while detecting collisions, and on a collision send a jam signal, abort, and back off for a random time.

51
00:11:34,671 --> 00:11:46,553
Question 41. The cause is propagation delay. Another node may have started already, but its bits had not reached this node when it sensed. The diagram shows this.

52
00:11:47,003 --> 00:11:54,598
Question 43 asks for the purpose of each feature of CSMA/CD. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:11:55,048 --> 00:12:07,229
Carrier sensing: a node does not start while another node transmits. This reduces collisions. Wait: when the channel is busy, the node defers until the channel is idle.

54
00:12:07,679 --> 00:12:22,869
Collision detection while transmitting: the sender finds a collision quickly, so it can abort and waste less time. Jamming bits: 48 bits put enough energy on the medium so that all the other nodes detect the collision.

55
00:12:23,319 --> 00:12:33,452
Exponential backoff: each node waits a random time, so the colliding nodes choose different times. After more collisions, the interval is longer.

56
00:12:33,902 --> 00:12:40,323
Question 44 has six parts about taking turns protocols. Pause the video and try it.

57
00:12:40,773 --> 00:13:02,597
Part a. The nodes take turns to use the channel, and nodes with more to send can take longer turns. Part b. In polling, the master gives turns to the slaves. In token passing, the node that holds the token has the turn. Part c. The node transmits its data. With nothing to send, it passes the turn on.

58
00:13:03,047 --> 00:13:17,853
Part d. The two strategies are polling and token passing. Part e. Polling has a central master that invites each node. Token passing has no master. A control token passes from node to node.

59
00:13:18,303 --> 00:13:26,025
Part f. The drawbacks are overhead, latency, and a single point of failure: the master, or a corrupted token.

60
00:13:26,475 --> 00:13:44,758
To recap. CSMA senses the channel first, but collisions still occur because of propagation delay. CSMA/CD also aborts on a collision, sends a jam signal and backs off for a random time, which doubles after each collision.

61
00:13:45,208 --> 00:14:02,445
Its efficiency is high when the propagation delay is small compared to the frame time. The receiver checks the CRC and sends no ACK. Taking turns uses polling or a token. The next video covers MAC addresses, ARP and Ethernet.
